\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Lineage of automated NLG evaluation. The field has evolved from holistic similarity metrics toward multi-dimensional LLM scoring. To support artefact-level construct claims, isolated criteria require recomposition. Current recomposition strategies diverge between black-box aggregation, which optimizes alignment with human rating distributions at the expense of explainability, and explainable aggregation, which estimates transparent criterion weights. The present thesis extends the latter path by treating recomposition as a construct-specification problem: theoretically specified criteria are recomposed under an explicit measurement model and interpreted as supporting a construct-level measurement claim.}}{10}{}\protected@file@percent }
